HashiCorp Vault
3-Node HA Cluster
Manual Setup Guide + Ansible Migration Notes

Stack Component	Value
Vault Nodes	vault1, vault2, vault3
Storage Backend	Raft (Integrated Storage)
Auto-Unseal	AWS KMS
TLS / CA	FreeIPA CA
Identity	FreeIPA / LDAP Auth Method
Credential Source of Truth	AWS Secrets Manager
Credential delivery to nodes	Ansible → EnvironmentFile
Container type	Proxmox LXC

 
Architecture Overview
This guide covers a 3-node HashiCorp Vault HA cluster using Raft integrated storage. Every node is equal in configuration — only node_id, api_addr, and cluster_addr differ per node. Vault auto-unseals via AWS KMS on every start, requiring no human intervention.

⚡ IMPORTANT: Each node unseals independently by calling AWS KMS directly. This means ALL 3 nodes need AWS credentials locally — not just the leader. When a standby takes over after a leader failure, it must unseal itself without any help from the other nodes.

The Bootstrap Flow (Credential Chain)
This is the pattern that simulates real-world bare metal Vault deployments:

AWS SM  (source of truth for KMS IAM credentials)
   ↓  Ansible retrieves at deploy time
   ↓  Writes /etc/vault.d/vault-env on each node  (chmod 600, vault:vault)
   ↓  Systemd loads as EnvironmentFile on vault.service start
   ↓  Vault process calls AWS KMS using those env vars
   ↓  KMS decrypts master key → node unseals
   ↓  Node joins Raft cluster as leader or follower

For now you will do steps 2-3 manually. The Ansible section at the end shows how to automate this once the manual deployment is validated.

 
PHASE 1 — AWS Setup (from your workstation)
Run these from your workstation or any machine that already has AWS CLI configured. You do NOT need AWS CLI on the Vault LXC nodes.

Step 1.1 — Create a Dedicated IAM User for Vault Unseal
In AWS Console → IAM → Users → Create user. Name it:

vault-unseal-homelab

Do NOT attach any managed policies. You will attach a minimal inline policy below.

Step 1.2 — Attach Inline KMS Policy
In IAM → Users → vault-unseal-homelab → Add inline policy. Paste the following JSON. Replace KEY_ARN with your actual KMS key ARN.

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "kms:Encrypt",
        "kms:Decrypt",
        "kms:DescribeKey"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:kms:YOUR_REGION:YOUR_ACCOUNT_ID:key/YOUR_KEY_ID"
    }
  ]
}

⚡ IMPORTANT: Scope the Resource to your exact KMS key ARN — never use '*'. This limits blast radius if the credentials are ever leaked.

Step 1.3 — Generate Access Key
IAM → Users → vault-unseal-homelab → Security credentials → Create access key. Choose 'Other' as use case. Save the Access Key ID and Secret Access Key — you only see the secret once.

Step 1.4 — Store Credentials in AWS Secrets Manager
From your workstation (AWS CLI needed here only):

aws secretsmanager create-secret \
  --name 'homelab/vault/kms-unseal-creds' \
  --description 'IAM credentials for Vault KMS auto-unseal' \
  --secret-string '{
    "aws_access_key_id": "AKIA...",
    "aws_secret_access_key": "wJalr...",
    "aws_region": "eu-west-1",
    "kms_key_id": "alias/vault-unseal"
  }' \
  --region eu-west-1

This is your source of truth. Ansible will pull from here later. The file on each node is ephemeral and can be regenerated at any time from this SM secret.

 
PHASE 2 — Node Preparation (All 3 Nodes)
Run everything in this phase on vault1, vault2, and vault3 — unless noted otherwise.

Step 2.1 — Set Hostname and /etc/hosts
On vault1:

hostnamectl set-hostname vault1

On vault2:
hostnamectl set-hostname vault2

On vault3:
hostnamectl set-hostname vault3

On ALL 3 nodes — add peer entries to /etc/hosts (replace IPs with your actual IPs):

cat >> /etc/hosts << 'EOF'
192.168.X.11  vault1
192.168.X.12  vault2
192.168.X.13  vault3
EOF

Step 2.2 — Install Vault (All 3 Nodes)
For Rocky Linux / RHEL:

dnf install -y dnf-utils
dnf config-manager --add-repo https://rpm.releases.hashicorp.com/RHEL/hashicorp.repo
dnf install -y vault
vault version

For Ubuntu / Debian:

apt-get install -y gpg curl
curl -fsSL https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com/gpg | gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg
echo "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg] \
  https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com $(lsb_release -cs) main" | tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/hashicorp.list
apt-get update && apt-get install -y vault
vault version

Step 2.3 — Create vault User and Directories (All 3 Nodes)

# Create system user (no shell, no home login)
useradd --system --home /etc/vault.d --shell /bin/false vault

# Create required directories
mkdir -p /opt/vault/data        # Raft storage
mkdir -p /etc/vault.d           # Config + TLS + env file
mkdir -p /var/log/vault         # Logs

# Set ownership
chown -R vault:vault /opt/vault /etc/vault.d /var/log/vault
chmod 750 /opt/vault/data
chmod 750 /etc/vault.d

Step 2.4 — Deploy FreeIPA CA Certificate and Vault TLS Certs (All 3 Nodes)
Each node needs: its own cert+key pair (issued by IPA CA), and the IPA CA cert. Request certs from IPA for each node if not already done.

On your IPA server or workstation:

# Request cert for vault1 (repeat for vault2, vault3)
ipa-getcert request \
  -f /etc/ipa/vault1.crt \
  -k /etc/ipa/vault1.key \
  -K host/vault1.yourdomain.com \
  -N CN=vault1.yourdomain.com

Copy to each node (adjust paths to match your IPA setup):

# On vault1
scp vault1.crt vault1:/etc/vault.d/vault.crt
scp vault1.key vault1:/etc/vault.d/vault.key
scp /etc/ipa/ca.crt vault1:/etc/vault.d/ca.crt

# On vault2
scp vault2.crt vault2:/etc/vault.d/vault.crt
scp vault2.key vault2:/etc/vault.d/vault.key
scp /etc/ipa/ca.crt vault2:/etc/vault.d/ca.crt

# On vault3
scp vault3.crt vault3:/etc/vault.d/vault.crt
scp vault3.key vault3:/etc/vault.d/vault.key
scp /etc/ipa/ca.crt vault3:/etc/vault.d/ca.crt

On each node after copying:

chown vault:vault /etc/vault.d/vault.crt /etc/vault.d/vault.key /etc/vault.d/ca.crt
chmod 640 /etc/vault.d/vault.key
chmod 644 /etc/vault.d/vault.crt /etc/vault.d/ca.crt

Step 2.5 — Create the AWS Credentials EnvironmentFile (All 3 Nodes)
This is the manual step that Ansible will automate later. The values come from what you saved in AWS SM in Step 1.4.

cat > /etc/vault.d/vault-env << 'EOF'
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=AKIA...
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=wJalr...
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=eu-west-1
EOF

chown vault:vault /etc/vault.d/vault-env
chmod 600 /etc/vault.d/vault-env

⚡ IMPORTANT: This file must exist on all 3 nodes with identical content. Each node calls KMS independently. If a standby node is missing this file, it will fail to unseal after a restart.

 
PHASE 3 — Vault Configuration (Per Node)
The config file is identical on all 3 nodes except for 3 values: node_id, api_addr, and cluster_addr. All other settings are the same.

Step 3.1 — Write vault.hcl on vault1

cat > /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl << 'EOF'
# ── General ────────────────────────────────────────────────
ui            = true
disable_mlock = true   # Required in LXC containers
cluster_name  = "homelab-vault"
log_level     = "info"
log_file      = "/var/log/vault/vault.log"

# ── Raft Integrated Storage ─────────────────────────────────
storage "raft" {
  path    = "/opt/vault/data"
  node_id = "vault1"

  retry_join {
    leader_api_addr     = "https://vault1:8200"
    leader_ca_cert_file = "/etc/vault.d/ca.crt"
  }
  retry_join {
    leader_api_addr     = "https://vault2:8200"
    leader_ca_cert_file = "/etc/vault.d/ca.crt"
  }
  retry_join {
    leader_api_addr     = "https://vault3:8200"
    leader_ca_cert_file = "/etc/vault.d/ca.crt"
  }
}

# ── Listener ────────────────────────────────────────────────
listener "tcp" {
  address            = "0.0.0.0:8200"
  cluster_address    = "0.0.0.0:8201"
  tls_cert_file      = "/etc/vault.d/vault.crt"
  tls_key_file       = "/etc/vault.d/vault.key"
  tls_client_ca_file = "/etc/vault.d/ca.crt"
}

# ── AWS KMS Auto-Unseal ─────────────────────────────────────
seal "awskms" {
  region     = "eu-west-1"
  kms_key_id = "alias/vault-unseal"
}

# ── Cluster Addressing ──────────────────────────────────────
api_addr     = "https://vault1:8200"
cluster_addr = "https://vault1:8201"
EOF

Step 3.2 — Write vault.hcl on vault2
Same file, 3 values changed:

# Only these 3 lines differ from vault1:
  node_id = "vault2"
api_addr     = "https://vault2:8200"
cluster_addr = "https://vault2:8201"

Step 3.3 — Write vault.hcl on vault3

# Only these 3 lines differ from vault1:
  node_id = "vault3"
api_addr     = "https://vault3:8200"
cluster_addr = "https://vault3:8201"

Step 3.4 — Set Config File Permissions (All 3 Nodes)

chown vault:vault /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl
chmod 640 /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl

 
PHASE 4 — Systemd Service (All 3 Nodes)

Step 4.1 — Create vault.service

cat > /etc/systemd/system/vault.service << 'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=HashiCorp Vault
Documentation=https://www.vaultproject.io/docs/
Requires=network-online.target
After=network-online.target
ConditionFileNotEmpty=/etc/vault.d/vault.hcl

[Service]
User=vault
Group=vault
ProtectSystem=full
ProtectHome=read-only
PrivateTmp=yes
PrivateDevices=yes
SecureBits=keep-caps
AmbientCapabilities=CAP_IPC_LOCK
CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_SYSLOG CAP_IPC_LOCK
NoNewPrivileges=yes

# AWS credentials for KMS auto-unseal
EnvironmentFile=/etc/vault.d/vault-env

ExecStart=/usr/bin/vault server -config=/etc/vault.d/vault.hcl
ExecReload=/bin/kill --signal HUP $MAINPID
KillMode=process
KillSignal=SIGINT
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5
TimeoutStopSec=30
LimitNOFILE=65536
LimitMEMLOCK=infinity
StandardOutput=append:/var/log/vault/vault.log
StandardError=append:/var/log/vault/vault.log

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

Step 4.2 — Enable and Start (All 3 Nodes)

systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl enable vault
systemctl start vault

# Verify it started
systemctl status vault
journalctl -u vault -f --no-pager   # watch live logs

 
PHASE 5 — Initialize and Verify Cluster

⚡ IMPORTANT: Run initialization ONCE from vault1 only. vault2 and vault3 join automatically via retry_join.

Step 5.1 — Set Shell Environment (vault1)

export VAULT_ADDR='https://vault1:8200'
export VAULT_CACERT='/etc/vault.d/ca.crt'

# Verify vault1 is reachable and sealed
vault status

Step 5.2 — Initialize Vault (vault1 ONLY)
With KMS auto-unseal, the unseal keys become recovery keys. You will not use them for day-to-day operations, but keep them safe — they are your disaster recovery mechanism if KMS becomes unavailable.

vault operator init \
  -recovery-shares=5 \
  -recovery-threshold=3 \
  -format=json > /tmp/vault-init.json

# View the output
cat /tmp/vault-init.json

Step 5.3 — Save Init Output to AWS Secrets Manager

# From your workstation — copy vault-init.json there first
scp vault1:/tmp/vault-init.json ./vault-init.json

aws secretsmanager create-secret \
  --name 'homelab/vault/init' \
  --description 'Vault init output — recovery keys and root token' \
  --secret-string file://vault-init.json \
  --region eu-west-1

# Delete local copies
rm ./vault-init.json
ssh vault1 'rm /tmp/vault-init.json'

⚠ WARNING: Treat the root token as a break-glass credential. Do not use it for day-to-day operations. Create admin policies and human-facing auth methods first, then revoke the root token.

Step 5.4 — Verify All 3 Nodes are Unsealed

# Check each node individually
VAULT_ADDR=https://vault1:8200 VAULT_CACERT=/etc/vault.d/ca.crt vault status
VAULT_ADDR=https://vault2:8200 VAULT_CACERT=/etc/vault.d/ca.crt vault status
VAULT_ADDR=https://vault3:8200 VAULT_CACERT=/etc/vault.d/ca.crt vault status

# Expected output on each:
# Sealed:     false
# HA Enabled: true
# HA Mode:    active   (on leader) / standby  (on followers)

Step 5.5 — Verify Raft Peer List

export VAULT_ADDR='https://vault1:8200'
export VAULT_CACERT='/etc/vault.d/ca.crt'
export VAULT_TOKEN='<root-token-from-init-json>'

vault operator raft list-peers

# Expected:
# Node     Address          State     Voter
# vault1   vault1:8201      leader    true
# vault2   vault2:8201      follower  true
# vault3   vault3:8201      follower  true

Step 5.6 — Enable Audit Logging

vault audit enable file file_path=/var/log/vault/audit.log
vault audit list

 
PHASE 6 — Firewall Rules (All 3 Nodes)

Step 6.1 — Open Required Ports

# API port — allow from your management VLAN and client networks
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-rich-rule='rule family=ipv4 source address=192.168.X.0/24 port port=8200 protocol=tcp accept'

# Cluster port — allow ONLY between vault nodes
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-rich-rule='rule family=ipv4 source address=192.168.X.11/32 port port=8201 protocol=tcp accept'
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-rich-rule='rule family=ipv4 source address=192.168.X.12/32 port port=8201 protocol=tcp accept'
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-rich-rule='rule family=ipv4 source address=192.168.X.13/32 port port=8201 protocol=tcp accept'

firewall-cmd --reload
firewall-cmd --list-all

 
PHASE 7 — Quick Reference

What Changes Per Node

Setting	vault1	vault2	vault3
node_id	vault1	vault2	vault3
api_addr	https://vault1:8200	https://vault2:8200	https://vault3:8200
cluster_addr	https://vault1:8201	https://vault2:8201	https://vault3:8201
TLS cert	vault1.crt / .key	vault2.crt / .key	vault3.crt / .key

Day-2 Commands

# Check cluster status
vault operator raft list-peers

# Check seal status on all nodes
for node in vault1 vault2 vault3; do
  echo "--- $node ---"
  VAULT_ADDR=https://$node:8200 vault status 2>&1 | grep -E 'Sealed|HA Mode'
done

# Rotate root token (do this after initial setup)
vault operator generate-root   # follow interactive flow

# Take a Raft snapshot
vault operator raft snapshot save /tmp/vault-snapshot-$(date +%Y%m%d).snap

# Watch live audit log
tail -f /var/log/vault/audit.log | jq .

 
ANSIBLE MIGRATION NOTES
Once the manual deployment is validated, use this section to convert each phase into Ansible. The structure maps directly to your existing site.yml pattern.

⚡ IMPORTANT: Keep this section as your reference. Do NOT start Ansible work until the manual cluster is fully operational and you have verified raft list-peers shows all 3 nodes.

Inventory Structure

# inventory/hosts.yml
all:
  children:
    vault_nodes:
      hosts:
        vault1:
          ansible_host: 192.168.X.11
          vault_node_id: vault1
          vault_api_addr: https://vault1:8200
          vault_cluster_addr: https://vault1:8201
        vault2:
          ansible_host: 192.168.X.12
          vault_node_id: vault2
          vault_api_addr: https://vault2:8200
          vault_cluster_addr: https://vault2:8201
        vault3:
          ansible_host: 192.168.X.13
          vault_node_id: vault3
          vault_api_addr: https://vault3:8200
          vault_cluster_addr: https://vault3:8201

Credential Bootstrap Task (Key Ansible Step)
This is the task that replaces the manual Step 2.5. Ansible retrieves credentials from AWS SM and writes the EnvironmentFile on each node — credentials never touch your git repo.

# tasks/vault_env.yml
- name: Retrieve KMS unseal credentials from AWS SM
  community.aws.aws_secret:
    name: homelab/vault/kms-unseal-creds
    region: eu-west-1
  register: vault_kms_secret
  delegate_to: localhost
  run_once: true
  no_log: true

- name: Write vault-env EnvironmentFile on each node
  ansible.builtin.template:
    src: vault-env.j2
    dest: /etc/vault.d/vault-env
    owner: vault
    group: vault
    mode: '0600'
  no_log: true

# templates/vault-env.j2
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID={{ (vault_kms_secret.secret | from_json).aws_access_key_id }}
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY={{ (vault_kms_secret.secret | from_json).aws_secret_access_key }}
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION={{ (vault_kms_secret.secret | from_json).aws_region }}

vault.hcl Template

# templates/vault.hcl.j2
ui            = true
disable_mlock = true
cluster_name  = "homelab-vault"
log_level     = "info"
log_file      = "/var/log/vault/vault.log"

storage "raft" {
  path    = "/opt/vault/data"
  node_id = "{{ vault_node_id }}"

{% for host in groups['vault_nodes'] %}
  retry_join {
    leader_api_addr     = "{{ hostvars[host].vault_api_addr }}"
    leader_ca_cert_file = "/etc/vault.d/ca.crt"
  }
{% endfor %}
}

listener "tcp" {
  address            = "0.0.0.0:8200"
  cluster_address    = "0.0.0.0:8201"
  tls_cert_file      = "/etc/vault.d/vault.crt"
  tls_key_file       = "/etc/vault.d/vault.key"
  tls_client_ca_file = "/etc/vault.d/ca.crt"
}

seal "awskms" {
  region     = "eu-west-1"
  kms_key_id = "alias/vault-unseal"
}

api_addr     = "{{ vault_api_addr }}"
cluster_addr = "{{ vault_cluster_addr }}"

Recommended Ansible Play Order in site.yml

# site.yml
- name: Install and configure Vault
  hosts: vault_nodes
  become: true
  roles:
    - vault_prereqs       # users, dirs, packages
    - vault_tls           # deploy IPA-issued certs
    - vault_env           # fetch from AWS SM → write EnvironmentFile
    - vault_config        # render vault.hcl from template
    - vault_service       # deploy and start systemd unit

# Run init separately — only once, only on vault1
- name: Initialize Vault cluster
  hosts: vault1
  become: true
  roles:
    - vault_init          # vault operator init + save to AWS SM


vault1  ·  vault2  ·  vault3  —  Raft HA  ·  AWS KMS Auto-Unseal  ·  FreeIPA CA
